<LegalLayout
  locale="en"
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Engram (Conversational Flashcards) collects, uses, and shares your information."
  effective="November 16, 2026"
  selfPath="/en/privacy"
  altPath="/privacy"
>
  <p class="lead">
    Engram is a voice study app: a tutor asks you your flashcards out loud and
    an AI grades your spoken answers. The decks can be ones you make in Engram,
    ready-made decks included in the app or, if you use it, your AnkiDroid
    collection. This policy explains what information the app
    handles, why, and who it is shared with. We try to collect as little as the
    app needs to work.
  </p>

  <div class="summary">
    <p><strong>The short version.</strong> We use Google Sign-In to create your account, and we store your plan and how many voice minutes you used this month. During a study session the text of each card is sent to ElevenLabs to record the tutor's voice reading it, the recording of each spoken answer goes through our server to OpenAI to be transcribed, and the transcript, together with the card, is sent to TypeSafe (Jev) to grade it. If you build a deck with AI, the request and files you choose are sent to OpenAI. We do not store your voice. Payments are handled entirely by Google Play. We do not sell your data. You can delete your account in the app.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    Engram (also presented as "Conversational Flashcards" / "Flashcards
    Conversacionales") is an Android application developed by Tobías Pardo
    (UTN Facultad Regional Delta, Argentina). In this policy, "Engram", "we",
    "us", and "our" refer to this developer. You can reach us at
    <a href="mailto:support@engramcards.com">support@engramcards.com</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Information we collect</h2>

  <h3>Account information</h3>
  <p>
    To use the AI tutor you sign in with Google. Through Firebase Authentication
    we receive your name, email address, profile photo URL, and a Google account
    identifier. We use this to create and secure your account and to recognize
    you across devices.
  </p>

  <h3>Voice and audio</h3>
  <p>
    The tutor speaks with recorded clips, not in a live conversation (see
    "Recorded voice clips"). While a session is active, the app listens to your
    microphone after the tutor finishes speaking. When it detects that you have
    finished answering, it sends that recording of your answer through our
    server (Google Cloud Functions) to <strong>OpenAI</strong>
    (gpt-4o-mini-transcribe), which returns the text of what you said.
    Microphone capture happens <strong>only during an active session</strong>
    and stops when the session ends. <strong>Our server passes the audio on
    without saving it, and we do not record or store your voice.</strong> OpenAI
    processes the audio under its own terms (see "Third-party services").
  </p>

  <h3>Flashcard content</h3>
  <p>
    You can study decks you make in Engram (by hand or with AI), ready-made
    decks included in the app and, with your explicit on-device permission,
    your AnkiDroid decks. AnkiDroid is optional. Engram reads AnkiDroid decks
    through AnkiDroid's official API: the front and back text of due cards and
    their scheduling information. Decks you make or copy into Engram, and their
    review schedule, are saved only on your device.
  </p>
  <p>
    To study a card, its text is sent through our server to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ElevenLabs</strong>, to record the tutor reading the question and the answer, and the hint if you ask for one. This happens the first time the card is studied, a few cards ahead during a session, or when you download a deck's audio.</li>
    <li><strong>TypeSafe (Jev)</strong>, together with the transcript of your answer, to grade it or to recognize a voice command (repeat, hint, next card, end).</li>
    <li><strong>OpenAI</strong>, only when you ask for a hint, to write it. We ask OpenAI not to store these requests.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you answer, the app saves the grade on your device: in your local
    AnkiDroid collection for AnkiDroid decks, and in Engram for the rest. We do
    not copy your deck library to our servers, and we do not sync to AnkiWeb
    (that stays a separate, user-initiated action inside AnkiDroid).
  </p>

  <h3>Recorded voice clips</h3>
  <p>
    Each clip is an audio file (MP3) made by ElevenLabs from the text of a card,
    a hint or one of the tutor's prewritten phrases. It contains the tutor's
    synthetic voice, never yours. On our side, clips are kept in Google Cloud
    Storage under a name computed from the text and the voice settings, with no
    link to your account: if someone else studies a card with exactly the same
    text, they receive the same clip and it is not recorded again. We have not
    set an expiry for these clips: today they are kept with no deletion date,
    and deleting your account does not remove them, since nothing ties them to
    it. On your phone, the app keeps the clips it has played or downloaded in
    its own storage until you uninstall the app or clear its data.
  </p>

  <h3>Decks you create with AI</h3>
  <p>
    When you use "Create with AI", the request you type, your answers to its
    questions, and any files you attach (PDF, slides, documents, images) are
    sent through our server to <strong>OpenAI</strong> to write the deck, and a
    summary of the request is sent to <strong>TypeSafe (Jev)</strong> to pick
    the deck's settings. We ask OpenAI not to store these requests, and we do
    not keep your files. The resulting deck is saved only on your device.
  </p>

  <h3>Plan and usage data</h3>
  <p>
    We store a small record tied to your account with your plan, your
    subscription status, how many voice minutes and AI decks you used each
    month, how many sessions you started (with the start time and the seconds
    charged for each), and how much each AI feature used per day (AI tokens and
    characters of text recorded as voice). These are counts only: no audio,
    transcripts or card text. This is held in Google Cloud Firestore and is what enforces your
    plan's monthly allowance. When you subscribe, Google Play sends us a
    purchase token so we can verify and unlock your subscription.
  </p>

  <h3>Feedback you send</h3>
  <p>
    If you send feedback from the app or rate a session, we store your message
    or rating with your account identifier and email address, the app
    version, your device's Android version and language, and the deck name
    when it is about a session, so we can reply and fix problems.
  </p>

  <h3>Usage analytics</h3>
  <p>
    To understand how the app is used and to fix problems, we collect product
    analytics through PostHog. These are usage events such as opening the app,
    starting and completing a session, selecting a deck (which includes the deck
    name), changing a setting, and subscription events. Events are associated
    with your account identifier. Analytics are disabled in development builds.
  </p>

  <h3>Technical and device data</h3>
  <p>
    Our service providers (Google Firebase and Google Play) automatically receive
    standard technical data needed to deliver and secure the app, such as app
    version, device and operating-system information. When the app crashes or
    hits an error, <strong>Firebase Crashlytics</strong> receives a crash report
    (the error, the app's state at that moment, device model and OS version)
    linked to your account identifier.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app also keeps a diagnostic log on your phone, which includes the text
    of the cards you study and the transcripts of your answers, so a problem in
    a session can be traced. It stays on the device and is not sent to us;
    files older than 14 days are deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>3. How we use your information</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To provide the core feature: a voice tutor over your decks.</li>
    <li>To create your account, sign you in, and keep your access in sync across devices.</li>
    <li>To apply your plan's monthly voice minutes and AI decks, and to verify and manage subscriptions.</li>
    <li>To understand usage, diagnose problems, and improve the app.</li>
    <li>To respond to your support requests.</li>
    <li>To comply with legal obligations and enforce our Terms of Use.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Third-party services</h2>
  <p>
    We rely on a small number of trusted providers to run Engram. Each processes
    data only as needed to provide its part of the service:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google (Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore, Cloud Functions, Cloud Storage, Crashlytics):</strong> account sign-in, plan and usage records, backend logic, storage of recorded voice clips, and crash reports.</li>
    <li><strong>ElevenLabs:</strong> recording the tutor's voice from the text of cards, hints and the tutor's prewritten phrases.</li>
    <li><strong>OpenAI:</strong> transcribing your spoken answers, writing hints when you ask for one, and writing decks you create with AI.</li>
    <li><strong>TypeSafe (Jev):</strong> grading your transcribed answers against the card, recognizing voice commands, and choosing settings for decks you create with AI.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Play Billing:</strong> processes all subscription payments. Engram never receives or stores your payment-card details.</li>
    <li><strong>PostHog:</strong> product usage analytics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These providers act as our processors or as independent controllers under
    their own policies. We do not sell your personal information, and we do not
    share it for cross-context behavioral advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Data retention</h2>
  <p>
    We do not keep your voice: each recorded answer is passed to OpenAI for
    transcription and discarded by our server. Recorded voice clips are kept as
    described in "Recorded voice clips". ElevenLabs, OpenAI and TypeSafe keep
    what they receive according to their own terms. Your account record, plan and usage counts are kept while your
    account exists so the service can function. Feedback is kept until it has
    been handled. Analytics events and crash reports are retained for a limited
    period. When you delete your account, we delete your account record, usage
    counts, feedback and purchase records right away (see "Your rights").
  </p>

  <h2>6. Security</h2>
  <p>
    Audio and data in transit are encrypted using the secure connections provided
    by our infrastructure. Access to backend data is restricted by security rules
    that let you read only your own account record and block direct client
    writes. No system is perfectly secure, but we take reasonable measures to
    protect your information.
  </p>

  <h2>7. International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Our providers (Google, ElevenLabs, OpenAI, TypeSafe and PostHog) operate globally, so your information may
    be processed on servers located outside your country of residence, including
    in the United States and the European Union. Where required, these transfers
    rely on the safeguards offered by those providers.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Your rights and choices</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live (for example under Argentina's Ley 25.326 or the
    EU GDPR), you may have the right to access, correct, export, or delete your
    personal data, and to object to or restrict certain processing. You can
    delete your account and the data tied to it at any time in the app
    (Account → Delete account), or by following the steps on our
    <a href="/en/delete-account">account deletion page</a>. To exercise any
    other right, email
    <a href="mailto:support@engramcards.com">support@engramcards.com</a>.
    You can also stop the app's data collection at any time by signing out,
    revoking the AnkiDroid or microphone permission in Android settings, or
    uninstalling the app. You manage or cancel your subscription directly in
    Google Play.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Permissions we request</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Microphone:</strong> to hear your spoken answers during a study session. Used only while a session is active.</li>
    <li><strong>AnkiDroid access (optional):</strong> a permission granted to AnkiDroid that lets Engram read your decks and write grades back. Only needed to study your AnkiDroid decks. You grant it at runtime and can revoke it.</li>
    <li><strong>Notifications:</strong> to show the call-style session notification while a session runs in the background, to tell you when a deck you asked the AI to write is ready, and for study reminders if you turn them on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>10. Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    Engram is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect
    personal information from them. If you believe a child has provided us data,
    contact us and we will remove it.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy as the app evolves. When we make material changes we
    will revise the effective date above and, where appropriate, notify you in the
    app or by email. Continued use after an update means you accept the revised
    policy.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy or your data? Email
    <a href="mailto:support@engramcards.com">support@engramcards.com</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
